      "The Prospectus",
      "Closed-end fund shares are sold one time, like a regular stock offering. After the initial public offering (IPO), they are listed and traded on an exchange. Open-end shares (mutual funds) are sold all the time and can be redeemed at any time by the fund's sponsor. They do not trade in the secondary market. They are nonnegotiable securities.\n\nBoth open-end and closed-end funds must file a registration statement with the SEC. Much of that statement becomes the fund's prospectus. A prospectus is a disclosure document. It gives full information about the fund, including its objectives, its managers, and its charges and fees.\n\nA prospectus must be current. If a prospectus is used for more than 9 months, the SEC does not allow its financial information to be more than 16 months old.\n\nMutual funds are continuous primary offerings. Every share sold is a new share bought straight from the issuer. So a buyer must always receive a prospectus at or before an offer or sale. A closed-end fund needs a prospectus only during its IPO and for a short time after. After that, shares trade between investors and no prospectus is needed.\n\nThere is also a separate document called the Statement of Additional Information (SAI). It has the fund's financial statements plus extra details not in the prospectus, such as the fund's history, its officers and directors, its holdings, and tax matters. The SAI includes:\n• A current schedule of investments\n• A current statement of assets and liabilities\n• A current income and expense statement\n• A current statement of changes in net assets\n• A current auditor's report (required for the year-end shareholder report)\n\nThe SAI is available to customers upon request. It is NOT required to be delivered with the summary prospectus at the time of sale.",
